{
  "id": "cecebre-edge-energy",
  "title": "Cecebre dam: data corruption and energy at the edge (reduced CSG)",
  "chapter": "Use case 7 — Edge gateway: data corruption and energy",
  "chapterOrder": 7,
  "formalism": "csg",
  "tool": "PRISM-games 3.x",
  "summary": "The water-level sensor of the Cecebre dam publishes through a SensiNact edge gateway; an attacker injects corrupted values at the southbound bridge. Compare synchronous and asynchronous communication, and the energy cost of the attack.",
  "description": [
    "Players: the water-level sensor (sen) publishes or skips a round; the attacker (att) injects a corrupted value or waits. The southbound bridge of the edge gateway is a non-player module: wl = 1 when the value forwarded to the cloud is corrupted.",
    "Synchronous bridge (MODE = 0): the message is handed over directly, nothing is stored. Asynchronous bridge (MODE = 1): corrupted payloads are kept in a buffer of capacity CAP (nb slots used) and one slot is freed per clean round. Scanning the buffer costs CPU time.",
    "Energy per round follows the power model, 2.1514·u + 4.142, where u is the gateway load (here: the corrupted value being handled plus the occupied buffer slots).",
    "Countermeasure: BLOCK = 1 closes the attacker's port. Scenario 1: p_att = 0.2; scenario 2: p_att = 0.8.",
    "Reduced teaching model (one sensor instead of three, no actuator): it reproduces the trends of the full study (asynchronous costs more, port blocking stabilises the energy), not its exact figures."
  ],
  "objectives": [
    "Map components to players, ports to actions and connectors to non-player modules.",
    "See how the communication style (sync / async) changes the cost of an attack.",
    "Observe the state-space growth when the buffer capacity increases."
  ],
  "exercises": [
    "Compute the maximal energy over 30 rounds for MODE = 0 and MODE = 1, with p_att = 0.2 then 0.8. Compute the sync / async ratio.",
    "Set BLOCK = 1: what is left of the attack?",
    "Increase CAP from 4 to 10 and note the number of states and the verification time."
  ],
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "k",
      "label": "Number of rounds",
      "value": "30"
    },
    {
      "name": "MODE",
      "label": "Communication",
      "value": "1",
      "description": "0 = synchronous, 1 = asynchronous"
    },
    {
      "name": "p_att",
      "label": "Attack success probability",
      "value": "0.2",
      "description": "scenario 1 = 0.2, scenario 2 = 0.8"
    },
    {
      "name": "CAP",
      "label": "Buffer capacity",
      "value": "4",
      "description": "asynchronous mode only (max 10)"
    },
    {
      "name": "BLOCK",
      "label": "Attacker port blocked",
      "value": "0",
      "description": "0 = no, 1 = yes (countermeasure)"
    }
  ],
